{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"next-cursor":"MTI2ODg3MTg5MTAwMCwxMC4xMDk4JTJGcnNwYi4yMDEwLjAwNTU=","total-results":33017,"items":[{"DOI":"10.1099\/mic.0.27437-0","title":["CRISPR elements in Yersinia pestis acquire new repeats by preferential uptake of bacteriophage DNA, and provide additional tools for evolutionary studies"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2005,3,1]]},"author":[{"given":"C.","family":"Pourcel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"GPMS, Institut de G\u00e9n\u00e9tique et Microbiologie, Universit\u00e9 Paris XI, 91405 Orsay cedex, France"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"G.","family":"Salvignol","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"GPMS, Institut de G\u00e9n\u00e9tique et Microbiologie, Universit\u00e9 Paris XI, 91405 Orsay cedex, France"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"G.","family":"Vergnaud","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Centre d'Etudes du Bouchet, 5 rue Lavoisier, 91710 Vert le Petit, France"},{"name":"GPMS, Institut de G\u00e9n\u00e9tique et Microbiologie, Universit\u00e9 Paris XI, 91405 Orsay cedex, France"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>\n                    The remarkable repetitive elements called CRISPRs (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats) consist of repeats interspaced with non-repetitive elements or \u2018spacers\u2019. CRISPRs are present in both archaea and bacteria, in association with genes involved in DNA recombination and repair. In the\n                    <jats:italic>Yersinia pestis<\/jats:italic>\n                    genome, three such elements are found at three distinct loci, one of them being highly polymorphic. The authors have sequenced a total of 109 alleles of the three\n                    <jats:italic>Y. pestis<\/jats:italic>\n                    CRISPRs and they describe 29 new spacers, most being specific to one isolate. In nine strains of\n                    <jats:italic>Yersinia pseudotuberculosis<\/jats:italic>\n                    , 132 spacers were found, of which only three are common to\n                    <jats:italic>Y. pestis<\/jats:italic>\n                    isolates. In\n                    <jats:italic>Y. pestis<\/jats:italic>\n                    of the Orientalis biovar investigated in detail here, deletion of motifs is observed but it appears that addition of new motifs to a common ancestral element is the most frequent event. This takes place at the three different loci, although at a higher rate in one of the loci, and the addition of new motifs is polarized. Interestingly, the most recently acquired spacers were found to have a homologue at another locus in the genome, the majority of these inside an inactive prophage. This is believed to be the first time that the origin of the spacers in CRISPR elements has been explained. The CRISPR structure provides a new and robust identification tool.\n                  <\/jats:p>","container-title":["Microbiology"],"is-referenced-by-count":1203},{"DOI":"10.1038\/nrmicro1142","title":["A CRISPR tool for Yersinia analysis?"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2005,4,1]]},"author":[{"given":"Sheilagh","family":"Molloy","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"container-title":["Nature Reviews Microbiology"],"is-referenced-by-count":0},{"DOI":"10.1371\/journal.pcbi.0010060.eor","title":["A guild of forty-five CRISPR-associated (Cas) protein families and multiple CRISPR\/Cas subtypes exist in prokaryotic genomes"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2005]]},"author":[{"given":"Daniel Henry","family":"Haft","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jeremy","family":"Selengut","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Emmanuel F","family":"Mongodin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Karen E","family":"Nelson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"container-title":["PLoS Computational Biology"],"is-referenced-by-count":2},{"DOI":"10.1371\/journal.pcbi.0010060","title":["A Guild of 45 CRISPR-Associated (Cas) Protein Families and Multiple CRISPR\/Cas Subtypes Exist in Prokaryotic Genomes"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2005,11,11]]},"author":[{"given":"Daniel H","family":"Haft","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jeremy","family":"Selengut","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Emmanuel F","family":"Mongodin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Karen E","family":"Nelson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"container-title":["PLoS Computational Biology"],"is-referenced-by-count":865},{"DOI":"10.1128\/jb.188.7.2364-2374.2006","title":["Chromosome Evolution in the\n                    <i>Thermotogales<\/i>\n                    : Large-Scale Inversions and Strain Diversification of CRISPR Sequences"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2006,4]]},"author":[{"given":"Robert T.","family":"DeBoy","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"The Institute for Genomic Research, 9712 Medical Center Drive, Rockville, Maryland 20850"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Emmanuel F.","family":"Mongodin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"The Institute for Genomic Research, 9712 Medical Center Drive, Rockville, Maryland 20850"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Joanne B.","family":"Emerson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"The Institute for Genomic Research, 9712 Medical Center Drive, Rockville, Maryland 20850"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Karen E.","family":"Nelson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"The Institute for Genomic Research, 9712 Medical Center Drive, Rockville, Maryland 20850"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    In the present study, the chromosomes of two members of the\n                    <jats:italic>Thermotogales<\/jats:italic>\n                    were compared. A whole-genome alignment of\n                    <jats:italic>Thermotoga maritima<\/jats:italic>\n                    MSB8 and\n                    <jats:italic>Thermotoga neapolitana<\/jats:italic>\n                    NS-E has revealed numerous large-scale DNA rearrangements, most of which are associated with CRISPR DNA repeats and\/or tRNA genes. These DNA rearrangements do not include the putative origin of DNA replication but move within the same replichore, i.e., the same replicating half of the chromosome (delimited by the replication origin and terminus). Based on cumulative GC skew analysis, both the\n                    <jats:italic>T. maritima<\/jats:italic>\n                    and\n                    <jats:italic>T. neapolitana<\/jats:italic>\n                    lineages contain one or two major inverted DNA segments. Also, based on PCR amplification and sequence analysis of the DNA joints that are associated with the major rearrangements, the overall chromosome architecture was found to be conserved at most DNA joints for other strains of\n                    <jats:italic>T. neapolitana<\/jats:italic>\n                    . Taken together, the results from this analysis suggest that the observed chromosomal rearrangements in the\n                    <jats:italic>Thermotogales<\/jats:italic>\n                    likely occurred by successive inversions after their divergence from a common ancestor and before strain diversification. Finally, sequence analysis shows that size polymorphisms in the DNA joints associated with CRISPRs can be explained by expansion and possibly contraction of the DNA repeat and spacer unit, providing a tool for discerning the relatedness of strains from different geographic locations.\n                  <\/jats:p>","container-title":["Journal of Bacteriology"],"is-referenced-by-count":61},{"DOI":"10.1186\/1471-2105-8-18","title":["PILER-CR: Fast and accurate identification of CRISPR repeats"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2007,1,20]]},"author":[{"given":"Robert C","family":"Edgar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"container-title":["BMC Bioinformatics"],"is-referenced-by-count":344},{"DOI":"10.1128\/jb.00187-07","title":["Regulation of\n                    <i>dev<\/i>\n                    , an Operon That Includes Genes Essential for\n                    <i>Myxococcus xanthus<\/i>\n                    Development and CRISPR-Associated Genes and Repeats"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2007,5,15]]},"author":[{"given":"Poorna","family":"Viswanathan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Michigan State University, East Lansing, Michigan 48824"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kimberly","family":"Murphy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Biology, Syracuse University, Syracuse, New York 13244"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Bryan","family":"Julien","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Allylix Inc., Lexington, Kentucky 40506"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anthony G.","family":"Garza","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Biology, Syracuse University, Syracuse, New York 13244"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lee","family":"Kroos","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Michigan State University, East Lansing, Michigan 48824"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Expression of\n                    <jats:italic>dev<\/jats:italic>\n                    genes is important for triggering spore differentiation inside\n                    <jats:italic>Myxococcus xanthus<\/jats:italic>\n                    fruiting bodies. DNA sequence analysis suggested that\n                    <jats:italic>dev<\/jats:italic>\n                    and\n                    <jats:italic>cas<\/jats:italic>\n                    (\n                    <jats:italic>C<\/jats:italic>\n                    RISPR-\n                    <jats:italic>as<\/jats:italic>\n                    sociated) genes are cotranscribed at the\n                    <jats:italic>dev<\/jats:italic>\n                    locus, which is adjacent to CRISPR (\n                    <jats:italic>c<\/jats:italic>\n                    lustered\n                    <jats:italic>r<\/jats:italic>\n                    egularly\n                    <jats:italic>i<\/jats:italic>\n                    nterspaced\n                    <jats:italic>s<\/jats:italic>\n                    hort\n                    <jats:italic>p<\/jats:italic>\n                    alindromic\n                    <jats:italic>r<\/jats:italic>\n                    epeats). Analysis of RNA from developing\n                    <jats:italic>M. xanthus<\/jats:italic>\n                    confirmed that\n                    <jats:italic>dev<\/jats:italic>\n                    and\n                    <jats:italic>cas<\/jats:italic>\n                    genes are cotranscribed with a short upstream gene and at least two repeats of the downstream CRISPR, forming the\n                    <jats:italic>dev<\/jats:italic>\n                    operon. The operon is subject to strong, negative autoregulation during development by DevS. The\n                    <jats:italic>dev<\/jats:italic>\n                    promoter was identified. Its \u221235 and \u221210 regions resemble those recognized by\n                    <jats:italic>M. xanthus<\/jats:italic>\n                    \u03c3\n                    <jats:sup>A<\/jats:sup>\n                    RNA polymerase, the homolog of\n                    <jats:italic>Escherichia coli<\/jats:italic>\n                    \u03c3\n                    <jats:sup>70<\/jats:sup>\n                    , but the spacer may be too long (20 bp); there is very little expression during growth. Induction during development relies on at least two positive regulatory elements located in the coding region of the next gene upstream. At least two positive regulatory elements and one negative element lie downstream of the\n                    <jats:italic>dev<\/jats:italic>\n                    promoter, such that the region controlling\n                    <jats:italic>dev<\/jats:italic>\n                    expression spans more than 1 kb. The results of testing different fragments for\n                    <jats:italic>dev<\/jats:italic>\n                    promoter activity in wild-type and\n                    <jats:italic>devS<\/jats:italic>\n                    mutant backgrounds strongly suggest that upstream and downstream regulatory elements interact functionally. Strikingly, the 37-bp sequence between the two CRISPR repeats that, minimally, are cotranscribed with\n                    <jats:italic>dev<\/jats:italic>\n                    and\n                    <jats:italic>cas<\/jats:italic>\n                    genes exactly matches a sequence in the bacteriophage Mx8\n                    <jats:italic>intP<\/jats:italic>\n                    gene, which encodes a form of the integrase needed for lysogenization of\n                    <jats:italic>M. xanthus<\/jats:italic>\n                    .\n                  <\/jats:p>","container-title":["Journal of Bacteriology"],"is-referenced-by-count":101},{"DOI":"10.1126\/science.1138140","title":["CRISPR Provides Acquired Resistance Against Viruses in Prokaryotes"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2007,3,23]]},"author":[{"given":"Rodolphe","family":"Barrangou","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Danisco USA Inc., 3329 Agriculture Drive, Madison, WI 53716, USA."},{"name":"Danisco France SAS, Boi\u0302te Postale 10, F-86220 Dange\u0301-Saint-Romain, France."},{"name":"De\u0301partement de Biochimie et de Microbiologie, Faculte\u0301 des Sciences et de Ge\u0301nie, Groupe de Recherche en Ecologie Buccale, Faculte\u0301 de Me\u0301decine Dentaire, Fe\u0301lix d'He\u0301relle Reference Center for Bacterial Viruses, Universite\u0301 Laval, G1K 7P4 Que\u0301bec, Canada."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Christophe","family":"Fremaux","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Danisco USA Inc., 3329 Agriculture Drive, Madison, WI 53716, USA."},{"name":"Danisco France SAS, Boi\u0302te Postale 10, F-86220 Dange\u0301-Saint-Romain, France."},{"name":"De\u0301partement de Biochimie et de Microbiologie, Faculte\u0301 des Sciences et de Ge\u0301nie, Groupe de Recherche en Ecologie Buccale, Faculte\u0301 de Me\u0301decine Dentaire, Fe\u0301lix d'He\u0301relle Reference Center for Bacterial Viruses, Universite\u0301 Laval, G1K 7P4 Que\u0301bec, Canada."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"He\u0301le\u0300ne","family":"Deveau","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Danisco USA Inc., 3329 Agriculture Drive, Madison, WI 53716, USA."},{"name":"Danisco France SAS, Boi\u0302te Postale 10, F-86220 Dange\u0301-Saint-Romain, France."},{"name":"De\u0301partement de Biochimie et de Microbiologie, Faculte\u0301 des Sciences et de Ge\u0301nie, Groupe de Recherche en Ecologie Buccale, Faculte\u0301 de Me\u0301decine Dentaire, Fe\u0301lix d'He\u0301relle Reference Center fo